nd on. Let me focus the implementation task on testutils and be careful about keeping the scope manageable within the constraints.\n\nThe DiffTest struct needs to capture the essential pieces for differential testing: input arguments, stdin, environment variables, expected exit code, and expected output. The core capabilities are executing two binaries side-by-side with identical inputs, comparing their outputs and exit codes, and reporting any divergences with enough context to debug. I should also support normalization hooks to handle cases where outputs differ in inconsequential ways.\n\nFor the PRD reference, I need to make an educated guess since I don't have the exact number. Looking at the naming pattern for rel00.0 components, testutils would likely be prd001 or similar, and I'll note that the stitch agent can resolve the correct file path. I'm realizing the test suite files might reference the PRD numbers, but since I can't access those files directly, I'll stick with prd004-testutils as my best guess based on the sequential numbering after prd003-format. Looking at the architecture components and their corresponding PRD numbers, there does seem to be a pattern—prd002 maps to sys, prd003 to format, and prd011 to magefiles—which suggests testutils likely follows right after format in the numbering scheme.\n\nI'll finalize the task definition for the differential testing harness that cmd packages will use to validate their Go implementations against GNU reference binaries, and I'll note in the required reading that the stitch agent should find whichever testutils PRD actually exists.\n\nGenerating task specification... Looking at the requirements and design decisions that need to be implemented, I need to build out the DiffTest struct with all the necessary fields for test configuration, then create the execution logic that runs both binaries in parallel and compares their outputs with support for normalization hooks to handle acceptable differences like time